==Question #12==
{{Cquote2|I think the community understands that you think reinforcing the predator vs prey dynamic is good. Can you tell us why? Clearly, a lot of the community doesn't it, and fundamentally a lot of the community votes no to content they don't like, which is the reason the polls exist. Why not, for instance, replace non-PvP incentives in the Wilderness with PvP incentives so that you still get the predator vs prey dynamic, but all parties involved are there because they like PvP?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H25pJMUl1_w&t=1699s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Bruno:''' I think part of the issue with this is that not everyone does like PvP, and this will just cause people who don't like PvP to still do it anyway, but maybe rig it like they have done previously in Bounty Hunter. We'll then end up with a system which is very strong if it's rigged, but it's fair if it's not rigged, so we can't really have that because people will rig it.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I think I'm looking at it from the perspective of "let's take out everything that isn't PvP related within the Wilderness, and make everything PvP related within the Wilderness.". I guess that's where you got your rigging part from, Bruno, is that if we did that, it’d be rigged. But we haven’t really looked to push the whole "predator vs. prey" dynamic that hugely within the Wilderness recently, at least not in the sense of “forced”.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I understand that Black chins are obviously a thing, and they give you the best experience if you’re not interrupted. But those were implemented quite literal years ago. I personally wouldn’t be opposed to looking at changing those either if it meant that that sentiment was removed.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' The other thing was the Wilderness Chaos Altar. You wouldn’t really need to use the Wilderness Chaos Altar unless you want to save money. Yes, it’s faster, but Gilded Altars still exist. It was meant to be balanced around that ethos anyway! So I think ultimately, the whole "predator vs. prey" dynamic: it is good, as long as the foundations that it’s built upon are also good, and that’s what we’ve been really trying to focus on recently.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We’ve introduced the option to prevent you from being able to get skulled, so that there’s no fear of skull tricking; you can always fight back with safety knowing that it’s quite literally impossible for you to skull if you don’t have one whilst that’s enabled. We introduced the PJ Timer to prevent you being attacked by multiple people in Singles. We also changed the Rev caves from Multi to Singles+, to again favour the solo player and make the battleground a little bit fairer, or even for both sides.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' The next initiatives that we’re looking towards are to try and bring people that want to PvP into PvP. The whole idea behind the PvP arena was “let’s have some rewards that are exclusive for PvP”. Of course, it’s a negative to those that don’t PvP because it’s going to mean that you could potentially get killed easier. But the premise of it was: PvP rewards from PvP content that are for PvP only, so that people can then practice PvP to get them and then go out and use them in PvP situations to then start getting better, and having that enjoyment and slowly building the community up that way.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Outside of that, the "predator vs. prey" dynamic is pretty much necessary for the economy of the Wilderness. Without it, there just aren’t enough people. If we have a small enough PvP community, then letting them fight among themselves, when there are hundreds of worlds now that can spread out non PvPers and PvPers as well, obviously isn’t great. So the alternative is to get more people into the Wilderness.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Our incentives for that now are less around the skilling side of things, and it’s more about monetary gain. They’ll look to be on par with other money makers within the game, so that you don’t have to go there. But if you want to, you can still make good money, knowing that you’re going to be susceptible to being attacked.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I think fundamentally, if a lot of the community don’t like that aspect - I know I’m going to get shot down for this, I can feel it already - but just don’t go there. There’s nothing that makes you go there, apart from the dragon pickaxe, and we’re aware of some of the other rewards from Wilderness bosses. But we’ve already said before that we’re going to look at removing the dragon pickaxe, and for the rings that the bosses drop, maybe we can look at alternatives for those as well.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Regardless of what happens, even if we didn’t look at "predator vs prey", and we were just looking at "predator vs predator" - if we added a new boss into the Wilderness, and it was meant to just be for PvPers, someone who doesn’t PvP is probably still going to want to go and kill that! So regardless of how we look at our design, that is always going to be there as a fundamental. People that want to look at collection log slots, that want to go for the pet, that maybe want to get the item just because it’s cool. That is always going to exist even if we are trying to focus on player versus player, rather than player versus prey.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So I don’t know what to say other than; if you don’t like predator vs prey, don’t go to the Wilderness, because you are prey unless you’re going in there as a predator, simple as that. It’s not something that we're going to look to change, because that is a core of Old School. PvP is essentially what the game was built upon. And yes, we’ve neglected it for years, but we’re looking to try and fix that now after years of looking at PvM, skilling, quests and everything else. So it’s not going anywhere.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We are very mindful, though, that there’s a large portion of players that don’t like the Wilderness. So that’s where we’re going to make sure that anything we do add in there is only really incentivising those that want to go, and it’s not going to make you feel as though you’re forced to go in. If you still feel that you’re forced to go in, then maybe start thinking about being prepared to go into the Wilderness knowing that you might go into a fight.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I said in a previous stream; you wouldn’t go into the Inferno and expect to defeat the Inferno if you’ve not even been able to get your fire cape first. You have to learn, you have to get good at the game in order to be able to beat Zuk. It’s the exact same for PvP. You can’t go into a PvP zone and expect to not be a prey and get absolutely trashed on if: 1. You’re not geared up properly, and 2. You’re not prepared on a mental level or a skill level.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Hopefully, as we continue working on Wilderness, and as we start bringing other things into there that aren’t as incentivised for those that feel they have to get forced into there for items. Maybe that mindset can shift to “okay, I’m going to make really good money, even if I’m not in the most efficient setup, so then I can fight back and now I’m not so much of a prey, or maybe I'm prey in disguise”. But that’s my bit on it.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I’m just going to say on the topic of being forced to go to the Wilderness - I think it comes down to the balancing being right, and holding our hands up and saying “I don’t think it is right across some of the places right now”. You mentioned the dragon pick as one reason why people, particularly Ironmen, will feel very forced to go there, because it’s such a clear upgrade to everything else.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think things like the Chaos Altar, it’s so much better. It’s not just an option, because it is such a fast upgrade. Even if you detest PvP, and have no interest in fighting back, even if you count the fact that you’re going to lose 30% of your bones if you’re bad, it’s still better!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Those players, I think, feel as though they’re being put in a situation where it’s the right choice, but they’ll not enjoy it at the same time. You’ll have players that have been directed to do stuff that they don’t like, but because it’s so good.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Now, that’s not to say we can’t solve this in the future, when it comes to how much gold/hour a boss is, or how much loot you get, or how much XP. If it’s that bit closer, is it then a valid option? If the Wilderness Altar wasn’t basically three times better than anything else, if that gap was less, then you do have a bit more of a trade off to say “Actually, I really don’t like the PvP situation, I will go and train Prayer at the Gilded Altar”.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think we can do better and get these things in the right place, so that you’re not forced. I saw a suggestion in the chat about a five black chin spot outside the Wilderness, because you still have that upgrade of a six-spot in the Wilderness - that narrows the gap a little bit, so that there is that choice again.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We can look at boss money in a similar sense. Outside the Wilderness, maybe you can get 5 million an hour, but in the Wilderness you can get that little bit more, so that you can say “I want the best money, sure, I’m willing to sacrifice it because I’m going to deal with PvPers.” if you want to do that. If you don’t, you can still do alright! So what I want to do is make it so that if you don’t really don’t like it, you don’t feel screwed. I’m looking at collection log gathering as kind of an aside as we’re not talking about the majority of players.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Playing devil's advocate here: what if I really don’t like training Agility? It’s the same thing. I’ve got to get my Agility levels up, so I’ve just got to get on with it.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I don’t like that! I don’t want players to be doing content that they don’t find fun, because that’s not good for the player. I don’t think that’s good for us! We want people to enjoy the game, we want people to stick around. So when I’m looking at Agility, I want to introduce skilling methods for agility that are not awful; that are relatively good. Sepulchre helps at the top end. I don’t want players to feel like they’re directed to content that they don’t personally enjoy, or is bad in the case of Ratcatchers.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It’s so much more nuanced when it comes to the Wilderness though, because it’s so much more subjective than simply “this is faster or slower”. It’s a bit of both. There’s a lot of different trade-offs, how good are you at PvP versus not?
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think what I’m also interested in is trying to get those scales right. If you see it as the prey on one side and the predator on the other side, how can you make that feel fairer than it does right now? I’d say we do very little on both sides right now. There’s not a huge incentive for PvPers, really. How much are you making, killing the people who’ve got a few dragon bones in their inventory? And for prey, apart from world-hopping, if you get attacked you’re basically dead in most cases, unless you really are trying to fight back.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' So I think if we can try and do more to strengthen both sides so that maybe we can get to a place where there is a healthier relationship, where dying doesn’t just feel inevitable, but fair. And that feeling is less one that’s negative, and moreso; “Okay, I understand that there’s some things I could have done better to avoid dying”. I don’t think that’s easy, for what it’s worth! I think these are genuinely hard challenges. We’re inevitably going have a lot of discussions internally about it. I’ve already had a lot of discussions, and these aren’t easy problems, because if they were easy we would have probably solved them already!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We’re looking at Wilderness bosses, these are going to be big topics when we look at those, and how we fix those, beyond just making them mechanically not just "safe spot then attack them". How do we make them better, for this whole predator/prey mechanic or not? I think what we need to do is agree on what course of action we want to take, and be able to actually start talking to players about how we are going to push forward with these other Wilderness changes down the line. We’re doing a PvP survey right now on certainly some of the priorities you might want to see in the near future.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Before we move to the next question, I just really get a chance to finish what I was saying, Kieren, because I feel you wanted to shut me down about my Agility comment! Where I was leading with it was that if I don’t like training Agility, I have to like it or lump it. But ultimately, if everyone started to feel like that, we would look to add alternate Agility training methods that would then maybe entice those that wouldn’t train it traditionally, to train it in another way, similar to Runecraft, with Guardians of the Rift, and now Giants’ Foundry for Smithing.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Maybe it’s not a case that we need to balance the Chaos Altar, maybe that could stay how it is. But there could be an alternative incentive outside of it that is better than the current Gilded Altars, for example, that might be a little bit different from ensouled heads, or the new demonic ash scattering, that then would take away that clear incentive that you say players have at the moment to go to the Chaos Altar, because it is so darn good.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' As a whole, I think the Chaos Altar is pretty bad anyway. As you said, people don’t make money from killing people there. And if you go there, you go there to die, you set yourself up with one invent and that’s your route to reduce the amount that you lose. So as a general design, it’s just awful all-round. So I think we should look to change that regardless. Maybe taking the incentives out with other designed content would be better.

==Question #13==
{{Cquote2|Where do you see Old School in 5 years? What would be some major long-term goals for the game as a whole moving forward?}}

